course: dictionary
lesson: 5801
title: A polite French title
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: «Mademoiselle» is a French word, and it is said like «mad-mwa-ZELL». It is a polite title, the French word for «Miss», used for a girl or a young woman who is not married.
explain: Look at the words around it to find its meaning. «Bonjour, mademoiselle» is a polite greeting, so you are saying good morning to a young lady. Swap in «Miss» and check: «Bonjour, Miss» still makes sense, so you have it right.
explain: Be careful, because the word is getting old. «Mademoiselle is an old word for miss; today madame is the safe word for any grown-up woman.» Many people in France now say «madame» to all women, and official forms no longer use «mademoiselle».
fact: Mademoiselle is a noun and a French word.
fact: The plural of «mademoiselle» is «mesdemoiselles», meaning more than one girl or young woman.
fact: «Mademoiselle» means Miss, a polite title for a girl or young unmarried woman.
fact: In English, «mademoiselle» is used in stories and jokes to make a character sound French, or for a French governess or teacher.
fact: «Madame» is the safe word for any grown-up woman today.
exercise: What does «mademoiselle» mean? | A teacher of maths | A kind of bread | *Miss, a polite title for a girl or young unmarried woman | A French dessert
exercise: «Mademoiselle is an old word for miss; today madame is the safe word for any grown-up woman.» Which word is the safe choice for any grown-up woman today? | Monsieur | *Madame | Mademoiselle | Mesdemoiselles
exercise: «Mademoiselle» has a plural form for more than one girl. Which is it? | *mesdemoiselles | madames | mademoiselle | mademoiselles
test: In the sentence «Bonjour, mademoiselle», what does mademoiselle mean? | Goodbye | Good morning | Lady of the house | *Miss
test: Why might an English story use «mademoiselle»? | To shout across a playground | To show the word is from Scotland | To say a number in maths | *To make a character sound French
exam: Jayden asks, «Is mademoiselle the safe word for any grown-up woman today?» What is the answer? | Yes, because it means teacher | *No, madame is the safe word, and official forms no longer use mademoiselle | No, because it means dinner | Yes, it is always the most polite word
